  "title": "Accounts Receivable",
  "description": "Learn how receivables arise, are recorded and collected.",
  "url": "/learn/intermediate/modules/2/lessons/3",
  "html": "<h2>What is Accounts Receivable?</h2> <p> Accounts Receivable represents amounts customers owe the company for goods or services provided on credit. </p> <div class=\"example-box\"> <h3>Credit sale</h3> <p> A company provides $5,000 of services on account. </p> <div class=\"journal-entry\"> <div class=\"journal-row journal-header\"> <div>Account</div> <div>Debit</div> <div>Credit</div> </div> <div class=\"journal-row\"> <div>Accounts Receivable</div> <div>$5,000</div> <div>—</div> </div> <div class=\"journal-row credit-row\"> <div>Service Revenue</div> <div>—</div> <div>$5,000</div> </div> </div> </div> <h2>Collection from customer</h2> <p> Later, the customer pays the $5,000. </p> <div class=\"journal-entry\"> <div class=\"journal-row journal-header\"> <div>Account</div> <div>Debit</div> <div>Credit</div> </div> <div class=\"journal-row\"> <div>Cash</div> <div>$5,000</div> <div>—</div> </div> <div class=\"journal-row credit-row\"> <div>Accounts Receivable</div> <div>—</div> <div>$5,000</div> </div> </div> <div class=\"key-points\"> <h3>Remember</h3> <p> Recording revenue does not always mean cash was received. </p> <p> Under accrual accounting, revenue can be recognized before collection. </p> </div> <h2>Gross vs net receivables</h2> <p> Companies may not expect to collect every dollar owed by customers. </p> <p> That leads us to the allowance for credit losses, which we'll study in the next lesson. </p>",
